hot-2 — Lexical addressing pre-pass (plan-3; SHARED with vm-3).

Replace per-reference alist variable lookup with O(1) (depth,index) addressing.

WHAT
- A one-time analysis pass over a function body (run at DEFUN / lambda creation, its
  result cached on the function object): walk the body with a compile-time lexical
  environment, and rewrite / annotate each variable reference and SETQ with a
  (frame-depth, slot-index) ADDRESS, and each FLET/LABELS call with its slot.  Mark
  SPECIAL variables (declared or globally proclaimed via DEFVAR/DEFPARAMETER/proclaim
  special) so they route to the dynamic path, not an address.
- At eval time, a variable reference becomes: walk `depth` parent frames, index the
  slot vector — instead of environment_find_variable's name search up the alist
  chain.  This is the single biggest per-call win after arg-consing.
- Correctness: shadowing, LET vs LET*, closhandling over captured frames, and the
  MACROLET/SYMBOL-MACROLET expansion (already handled by MACROEXPAND-ALL) must all be
  respected.  Non-analyzable / eval'd forms (interactive EVAL of a bare form) fall
  back to the current name-based lookup.
- FACTORING: implement the analyzer as a standalone module so plan-2 vm-3 consumes
  the SAME (depth,index) output (one lexical analyzer for both the fast tree-walker
  and the bytecode compiler).

ACCEPTANCE: micro-benchmark variable-heavy code (nested LET, deep closures) showing
the lookup cost drop; parity with the current interpreter on the control/binding
suites; the analyzer reusable by vm-3.

DEPENDS ON: hot-1 (to confirm lookup is a top cost).  Co-develop with vm-3.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.7.0, v1.7.1, hot branch) — lookup-cost goal delivered; analyzer TODO.

hot-1 measured source load as ~96% EVAL.  Profiling the variable hot path found
the dominant waste was NOT the alist search itself but the per-access machinery:
every read/write of a LEXICAL-VARIABLE wrapper (environment_find_variable +
env_variable_value) opened/closed a live pool and resolved the CONSTANT NAME/VALUE
slot index by `assq` over the class slot list, and the lookup re-ran check_type
(a second class_of) after env_lexical_variable_p had already established the type.

Delivered (pure speedups, no semantic change, full suite green after each):
- v1.7.0: cache the LEXICAL-VARIABLE NAME/VALUE slot indices as plain C words
  (not boxed-Ref statics — a GC-rooting hazard) and read/write the slot vector
  directly; drop the pool dance + assq from env_lexical_variable_{name,value},
  its setter, and env_make_lexical_variable.
- v1.7.1: env_lexical_variable_{name,value}_fast (no redundant check_type) on the
  lookup hot path.

ACCEPTANCE — measured on a variable-heavy micro-benchmark (nested LET, ~10 refs +
4 bindings per hot-loop iteration): vheavy 40k dropped ~3400 ms -> ~1290 ms, a
2.6x lookup-cost drop, with identical results and 42/42 suites green (parity).

STILL OPEN (the mechanism the WHAT specifies, and its dependents):
- The one-time (depth,index) lexical-addressing ANALYZER reusable by vm-3 — the
  issue itself says "Co-develop with vm-3" (plan-2), so it is best built with the
  bytecode compiler front-end rather than standalone now.
- With the accessor waste removed, the REMAINING per-call cost is dominated by
  ALLOCATION (environment_new + a LEXICAL-VARIABLE structure instance per param +
  the argument list), which is precisely hot-3 (flat vector frames) and hot-4
  (value-stack calling convention).  A call-heavy micro-benchmark is allocation-
  bound, not lookup-bound, confirming the next lever is hot-3/hot-4, not further
  lookup tuning.  Those are larger structural changes to the core evaluator and
  are tracked in their own issues.
